{# ============================================================================
   THE SOURCE RECORD, BESIDE THE FORM — the rail's first card.

   The thin strip at the top of the page proves the observation exists; it cannot
   be READ. Here the same record is legible: its own words, and the record itself —
   which patrol, whose eyes, when, where.

   WORDS AND FACTS, AND NOTHING ELSE. The filer is describing something they may
   not have seen, and what helps them is the sentence the observer wrote and the
   four facts that place it. A map of the pin answers "where" with a picture that
   has to be interpreted, when the Where row answers it in the notation the
   observation's own page uses; the photographs belong to the record that holds
   them and to the case file this filing becomes.

   NOTHING ON IT IS EDITABLE. Provenance is written once, at filing, and never
   again; there is not a control on this card and the seam test holds it to that.
   The tab names the record and stops — at 340px a longer one is clipped — and
   "not editable" rides on the label of the rows it is about.

   IT RENDERS ONLY WHERE THE FILING CAME FROM A RECORD. A walk-in filing has
   nothing to show, and the rail is the checklist alone.

   EVERY ROW IS CONDITIONAL, because the hand-off is a query string another module
   composes: a module with no patrol, no named observer or no coordinates sends
   what it has, and a row nobody was told about is absent rather than empty.

   Parameters: prefill
   ========================================================================== #}
<div class="c" data-incident-observation>
    <span class="tab">The observation<span class="src">· {{ prefill.label }}</span></span>

    {# THE OBSERVATION'S OWN WORDS, quoted — so it reads as somebody else's
       sentence rather than as this form's prefilled answer, which is the field
       below it and is editable. #}
    {% if prefill.note %}<p class="rr-quote">“{{ prefill.note }}”</p>{% endif %}

    <span class="rr-sub">The record itself · not editable</span>
    {% if prefill.patrol %}<div class="rln"><span>Patrol</span><span class="mono">{{ prefill.patrol }}</span></div>{% endif %}
    {% if prefill.ranger %}<div class="rln"><span>Recorded by</span><span class="mono">{{ prefill.ranger }}</span></div>{% endif %}
    {# THE MOMENT, AS A MACHINE INSTANT WITH A READABLE FALLBACK. `datetime` is the
       offset-qualified instant and the frame rewrites the TEXT of the element to the
       reader's own zone — which is the whole answer to "whose clock is this". An
       instant with no offset on it is one a browser resolves in its own zone, so
       `date('c')` is not optional. The text is what a reader with no JavaScript
       sees, and it is the register's own zone rather than the observer's: the form
       beside it says the same moment the same way, and two zones on one page is the
       confusion this element removes. #}
    {% if prefill.occurredAt %}
        <div class="rln"><span>When</span><span class="mono"><time datetime="{{ prefill.occurredAt|date('c') }}"
                data-localtime-format="daystamp">{{ prefill.occurredAt|date('D j M Y · H:i')|lower }}</time></span></div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if prefill.positionLabel %}
        <div class="rln"><span>Where</span><span class="mono">{{ prefill.positionLabel }}</span></div>
    {% endif %}
    {# THE SOURCE ROW IS THE WAY BACK TO EVERYTHING THIS CARD LEAVES OUT — the
       record's own position on a map, its photographs, its history. They are one
       click away on the page that owns them, which is where they are current;
       copied here they would be a second gallery to keep in step. The link is the
       one the hand-off carried, and a hand-off without one leaves the words. #}
    <div class="rln"><span>Source</span><span class="mono">
        {%- if prefill.backUrl %}<a href="{{ prefill.backUrl }}">{{ prefill.sourceLabel }}</a>{% else %}{{ prefill.sourceLabel }}{% endif %}
        · its position and its photographs are on it</span></div>

    <div class="use">The filer is describing something they may not have seen. Everything the person who did see it
        wrote down is one glance to the right, for as long as the form is open, and the observation itself is one
        click further.</div>
</div>
